PRIME TARGET — stdlib coupling on IsStagingName's digit guard:
- Read go1.26.3 stdlib source (GOROOT/src/os/tempfile.go). CreateTemp's
  doc comment guarantees only "adding a random string to the end"; the format
  (decimal, via strconv.FormatUint(runtime_rand(),10)) is an implementation
  detail, not a documented contract. Confirmed this is a real coupling to an
  unexported implementation detail, exactly as flagged.
- However this is NOT the same failure mode as attempt 2. Attempt 2's literal
  ".tmp" in SkipPredicate was never checked against what atomicWrite actually
  emitted, so drift was silent and undetected by any test. Here,
  TestSkipPredicate_StagingNames's "current form" case (snapshot_test.go:184)
  derives its fixture via a real os.CreateTemp(dir, base+storage.StagingSuffix
  +"*") call — not a hand-typed string. If a future Go release changed the
  random-suffix format (hex, base36, etc.), this exact test would start
  failing loudly on the next `go test` after the toolchain upgrade, not pass
  silently. The doc comment on StagingSuffix/IsStagingName explicitly states
  the assumption ("decimal random", with a worked example). Verdict: this is
  acceptable engineering with a documented, self-detecting assumption, not a
  silent repeat of the attempt-2 defect.
- Checked whether the digit guard is load-bearing or just decorative: traced
  CSVDirectory (dataloader.New) back to cfg.DataDirectory in cmd/server/
  main.go — user-provided bank-export CSVs live directly in the same DataDir
  that SkipPredicate walks, with arbitrary user-chosen filenames (accounts.go
  comment even shows "usaa-checking-2026.csv"). Without the digit guard, ANY
  file merely containing the substring ".tmp-" (e.g. a user's oddly-named
  export) would be silently excluded from backups and unprotected from
  restore pruning. This is a real, not hypothetical, risk class given this
  app's directory layout — the guard is genuinely load-bearing, not
  decorative.

